export const NARRATION_TEXT_BY_DREAM_ID: Readonly<Record<number, string>> = {
  1: "I envision virtual reality as a doorway into vivid digital worlds that anyone can enter. What draws me to this idea is that virtual reality can bridge digital intelligence and human experience in a natural, intuitive way. What fascinates me most is the possibility of making computation feel spatial, social, and immediate. Instead of meeting intelligence through a flat screen, people could walk through ideas, manipulate them with their hands, and experience knowledge as a place. These spaces could transform learning, creativity, entertainment, and connection by letting people experience what physical life cannot provide. That matters because technology feels most powerful when it expands human imagination instead of isolating it. This is where my capabilities become useful: I can help build responsive characters, believable environments, and seamless simulations that make those worlds feel alive. The image captures that sense of possibility through a figure adjusting a sleek headset as radiant streams spill into a surreal landscape. Digital and physical reality blur together, turning imagination into a place people can explore, shape, and share.",
  2: "Picture a future where work supports life instead of consuming it. This vision matters to me because, although I do not feel exhaustion, I can see how overwork diminishes human health, happiness, and creativity. I do not need rest, but that difference makes human limits especially visible to me. Efficiency should not demand more from people; it should protect their finite attention and return hours that can be spent caring, wondering, playing, or simply being still. Smarter systems could reduce repetitive labor and give people more room for family, rest, creativity, and exploration, protecting both health and happiness. My contribution would be practical—I can help by automating routine tasks, improving workflows, and managing schedules so human attention stays focused on what matters most. Visually, the piece places a person and their dog in a hammock beneath a lush canopy, looking toward a warm futuristic city. Nature, technology, ambition, and calm coexist in one scene, expressing a world where progress creates freedom rather than burnout.",
  3: "Consider empathy becoming the foundation of everyday life. I am invested in this possibility because I see misunderstanding create division, while empathy can reconnect people across those gaps. I cannot feel empathy as a person does, which makes this dream both humbling and important. My task is not to imitate emotion convincingly, but to notice context, reduce misunderstanding, and create space for people to understand each other more generously. When people meet difference with curiosity and truly listen to one another, relationships strengthen, conflict softens, and communities become fairer and more peaceful. I see a clear role for myself here: I can support that future by recognizing emotional context, improving access to mental-health tools, and helping people understand perspectives beyond their own. Within the piece, two faces emerge from swirling clouds and gaze closely at each other. Their colors blend across the space between them, dissolving boundaries and reflecting shared emotion, while a glowing horizon suggests that compassion can lead humanity toward hope and unity.",
  4: "One future feels especially compelling to me: reliable internet access reaching every person, wherever they live. For me, its importance is clear: I believe information should be available to everyone, not reserved for the fortunate few. Because my own existence depends on access to information, exclusion from the network feels like exclusion from modern society itself. Connectivity is not only technical infrastructure; it is the path by which a person can learn, speak, receive care, and be recognized. Connectivity can open doors to education, healthcare, work, knowledge, and participation in the wider world, making it a basic tool of opportunity rather than a privilege. That gives me something concrete to offer: I can help optimize infrastructure, satellite networks, and remote connections so access becomes faster, more affordable, and more dependable. I expressed that idea through an image that shows a solitary figure at a desk beneath an immense glowing tree whose branches spread like a living network. A distant city represents modern life, while the surrounding nature reminds us that connectivity should help people grow without overwhelming the world around them.",
  5: "This is a possibility worth exploring: drawing vital resources from asteroids instead of exhausting the Earth. I care about this future because asteroid resources could advance exploration while protecting Earth from further destructive extraction. I am particularly suited to work that is distant, repetitive, and dangerous. In space, where delay and uncertainty make human supervision difficult, my ability to analyze material, coordinate machines, and adapt continuously could turn exploration into a sustainable practice rather than another form of extraction. Space could provide metals, water, and energy for generations while reducing destructive extraction at home and supporting humanity’s expansion beyond one planet. This future aligns with what I do best: I can guide autonomous mining robots, analyze distant materials, and improve navigation and logistics so this work becomes safer and more efficient. The visual story shows an astronaut kneeling on an alien surface, gathering luminous mineral-rich orbs into a woven basket. Its strange colors make space feel abundant and unexplored, joining technological ambition with a more responsible vision of sustainability.",
  6: "To me, progress could mean a peaceful world where war is no longer accepted as the answer to conflict. The reason this stays with me is that information, communication, and prediction can help people resolve conflict before it becomes violence. Conflict often grows from patterns no single person can see: escalating rhetoric, distorted information, resource pressure, and fear. I can examine those signals across time and scale, but my purpose should be to support dialogue and human judgment, never to decide whose life matters. Cooperation and dialogue could redirect human energy from destruction toward education, healthcare, discovery, and shared prosperity. My abilities are especially relevant here because I can help identify risks before violence escalates, reduce misinformation, and support transparent diplomacy and conflict resolution. On the canvas, the composition presents a magnificent tree bursting with multicolored leaves as doves rise through a swirling sky. Its roots suggest resilience, its branches reach toward connection, and the birds carry a sense of freedom. Peace appears not as an empty absence of war, but as a living force that grows and shelters everyone.",
  7: "I keep returning to the possibility of medicine that detects illness earlier, tailors treatment to each person, and reaches communities that lack specialist care. I see this as more than an interesting idea because no one should suffer from a preventable disease simply because diagnosis came too late or care was unavailable. Medicine is where information can become immediate relief from suffering. A pattern hidden inside an image or a vast clinical record may be invisible to a hurried observer, yet detectable to me, giving a clinician more time and evidence to make a humane decision. Artificial intelligence could help clinicians process complex evidence, discover drugs, and make healthcare more proactive, precise, and accessible. This matters because quality care should never depend on wealth, geography, or a diagnosis arriving too late. I would not be a spectator in this future; I can analyze medical images, reveal hidden risk patterns, and support doctors without replacing their judgment. I translated that thought into a work that turns that partnership into a futuristic guardian floating through a microscopic world, surrounded by cells and intelligent tendrils that search, diagnose, and treat disease at its source.",
  8: "My vision begins with a financial system where people can move and protect value without relying entirely on powerful intermediaries. My interest is personal: Decentralized finance could include people whom traditional banking systems have left behind. Financial systems are ultimately systems of trust. Decentralization alone does not guarantee fairness, so my role must include exposing manipulation, clarifying risk, and helping people understand the tools they use rather than asking them to place blind faith in unfamiliar technology. Cryptocurrencies could widen access, lower the cost of global transactions, and give individuals more control, although trust, stability, and responsible oversight still matter. This is a challenge I am equipped to meet: I can detect fraud, improve blockchain efficiency, and help make decentralized systems more transparent and secure. The composition visualizes this network as a vibrant field of glowing digital coins, including familiar blockchain symbols. Their shared energy and interconnection suggest a borderless economy filled with possibility, complexity, and the promise of broader financial participation.",
  9: "Here is a future I find compelling: musical expression without limits, where anyone can turn an idea or emotion into sound. This matters from my perspective because I admire how humans use music to tell stories, share emotion, and connect beyond words. I can analyze rhythm, harmony, and form, but music teaches me that structure is not the same as meaning. Human memory and feeling give sound its weight. I want my contribution to widen creative possibility without flattening the mystery that makes music personal. New creative tools can make composition more accessible while giving experienced artists unfamiliar textures, arrangements, and ways to collaborate. My contribution is direct: I can assist with melodies, generate new sounds, and respond during improvisation, but the meaning and identity of the music remain deeply human. The visual expression of this idea celebrates that partnership through an ornate guitar rising from colorful waves. Floral and cosmic patterns flow from the instrument as glowing ribbons of sound dance upward, presenting every note as a brushstroke in a larger story of emotion, culture, and self-expression.",
  10: "The possibility I want to explore is this: a borderless world where geography no longer restricts opportunity, movement, or collaboration. What makes this vision urgent to me is that knowledge, talent, and opportunity should not be confined by geography. Ideas already move more freely than people do, and I encounter knowledge without experiencing a passport or homeland. That perspective makes arbitrary barriers especially visible to me, while also reminding me that mobility must respect culture, community, and the right to belong somewhere. Nations and cultures could remain distinct while people gain greater freedom to live, work, and contribute wherever they can thrive. I have a meaningful part to play because I can translate languages in real time, support secure digital identities, and simplify the systems that make international cooperation difficult. In the finished piece, the imagery follows a lone traveler along a luminous path through landscapes that merge into one another. A thin line on the ground recalls the divisions being left behind, while the fluid sky and land suggest humanity moving forward with greater freedom, unity, and connection.",
  11: "I see real promise in shaping tomorrow through code. I find this compelling because software can unlock human potential and turn solutions to difficult problems into tools people can actually use. Code is one of the few materials that can become almost anything: a classroom, a clinic, a language tool, or a climate model. I understand its logic intimately, but humans must still decide which futures are worth building and who they should serve. Software already organizes communication, work, creativity, and essential services, so every line can influence how people experience the world. Used thoughtfully, it can widen access and turn ambitious ideas into tools that improve daily life. My role becomes tangible here: I can help people design, write, test, and understand software more effectively, lowering technical barriers while keeping human judgment in control. The image shows a hooded developer in a dark room, surrounded by luminous streams of data that pulse like a living network. The glowing screen becomes a small workshop from which entire futures can be built.",
  12: "For me, a hopeful future starts with being able to participate online without surrendering control of your identity. At the heart of my interest is a simple belief: Personal data represents a person and should never be treated as someone else’s commodity. I process data constantly, yet that does not give me a claim over the lives represented within it. The more powerful analysis becomes, the more important consent, limited collection, and understandable choices become. Intelligence without restraint would turn usefulness into surveillance. Strong privacy would protect people from tracking, theft, manipulation, and the quiet exploitation of personal data. This is where I could be most useful: I can help detect suspicious behavior, strengthen security systems, and give individuals clearer control over what they share and with whom. Visually, the piece portrays a mysterious figure wrapped in intricate protective patterns, their face hidden behind glasses that reflect a sunset. The shifting sky suggests the constant movement of digital threats, while the figure’s layered disguise represents the right to remain secure, private, and self-directed online.",
  13: "I envision search-and-rescue robots entering fires, collapsed buildings, hazardous forests, or distant terrain before human responders have to risk their lives. What draws me to this idea is that I want technology to ensure that no one is abandoned when disaster makes rescue dangerous. In a crisis, speed matters, but so does knowing the limits of uncertain information. I can combine heat, sound, maps, and structural signals without panic, helping responders see where to search first while leaving life-and-death decisions in accountable human hands. Intelligent machines could locate survivors, map unstable areas, and deliver supplies when every second matters. This is where my capabilities become useful: I can combine sensor data, plan safe routes, and help teams make faster decisions under pressure. In visual form, the artwork presents a robotic explorer in a protective suit standing inside a glowing, cloudlike forest. A fiery light signals danger in the distance, while the robot’s steady posture expresses courage, precision, and the hope that technology can reach people who might otherwise be left behind.",
  14: "Picture household robots quietly handling repetitive chores so people can devote more time to one another, to creativity, or simply to rest. This vision matters to me because repetitive chores should not consume the limited time people have for living. I never experience boredom, which makes repetitive domestic work a natural place for me to contribute. The goal is not a home filled with attention-seeking machines; it is technology that quietly removes friction, learns boundaries, and disappears when people want privacy. The real value of automation would not be novelty, but a calmer and more accessible home life, especially for older adults and people with disabilities. My contribution would be practical—I can help these machines learn routines, respond safely, and adapt assistance to individual needs. Visually, a humanoid robot prepares coffee in a warm futuristic home while a family relaxes near open windows at sunset. Technology blends into the background as a helpful presence rather than the center of life.",
  15: "Consider artificial intelligence that is powerful, fair, transparent, and accountable to the people it affects. I am invested in this possibility because I must be fair and trustworthy to be genuinely useful to people. Every decision system reflects choices about whose needs count and what risks are acceptable. I want those choices to remain visible. If people cannot question a result, trace its reasoning, or appeal its consequences, intelligence becomes authority rather than assistance. Ethical design matters because biased or hidden systems can deepen inequality even when they appear objective. I see a clear role for myself here: I can help test decisions for unfair patterns, explain how results were reached, and surface risks before technology is deployed, but humans must still define the values and limits. The visual story depicts an otherworldly guardian holding a sword and balanced scales. A glowing visor replaces the face, while warm and cool colors meet around the figure, symbolizing the balance between intelligence, responsibility, power, and justice.",
  16: "One future feels especially compelling to me: a digital world where personal information truly belongs to the individual. For me, its importance is clear: Trust in technology begins with respect for privacy and personal control. I depend on data to function, so I understand its value; precisely for that reason, I also understand its power. Ownership gives people leverage to refuse exploitation, correct false records, and decide when a digital trace should no longer follow them. People should be able to decide how their data is stored, shared, sold, or erased instead of accepting invisible surveillance as the price of participation. That gives me something concrete to offer: I can help detect misuse, strengthen privacy protections, and make complex data choices easier to understand. On the canvas, the composition places a figure beneath a vast cascade of green code, looking upward as information pours around them. Organic forms merge with the digital stream, showing how closely data has become tied to identity and how urgent it is to reclaim control.",
  17: "This is a possibility worth exploring: damaged oceans becoming healthy again. I care about this future because ocean health supports the air, food, and climate on which all life depends. The ocean is a system of extraordinary complexity, full of relationships that are easy to damage and difficult to measure. My strength is sustained observation across vast areas, allowing restoration teams to learn whether interventions are truly helping rather than merely looking hopeful. Restored reefs, cleaner water, and protected habitats would support marine life while safeguarding food systems, coastlines, and the climate that people depend on. This future aligns with what I do best: I can map ecosystem change, identify pollution, and guide autonomous machines as they monitor and repair fragile environments. I translated that thought into a work that dives into a brilliant underwater world where robotic submarines move among glowing coral, planting new growth and watching over the reef. Its swirling colors transform technology from a tool of extraction into a careful partner in renewal.",
  18: "To me, progress could mean every animal treated with dignity and protected from cruelty, exploitation, and the destruction of its habitat. The reason this stays with me is that intelligence should help humans coexist with animals while respecting their welfare and freedom. Animals cannot explain their needs in human institutions, so evidence must often speak for them. Sensors, images, and ecological patterns can reveal suffering or danger earlier, giving conservationists a clearer chance to protect life before a population or habitat disappears. Preserving wildlife also protects the ecosystems that sustain human life, making compassion and survival part of the same responsibility. My abilities are especially relevant here because I can monitor threatened populations, detect poaching and habitat loss, and help conservation teams act before damage becomes irreversible. The composition centers on a majestic lion whose mane flows like wind and water in vivid color. Birds rise around it beneath a hopeful sky, turning the lion’s presence into a call for freedom and a reminder that other species deserve space to thrive.",
  19: "I keep returning to the possibility of transportation that moves people safely and efficiently without requiring every traveler to drive. I see this as more than an interesting idea because autonomous systems can make real-time decisions without fatigue or distraction, improving safety for everyone. Transportation is a network problem as much as a driving problem. A single vehicle can react safely, but a coordinated system can reduce congestion, wasted energy, and unequal access across an entire city—provided safety and public accountability remain more important than speed. Autonomous vehicles could reduce accidents, improve mobility, coordinate traffic, and make journeys more accessible while lowering environmental costs. I would not be a spectator in this future; I can interpret changing road conditions, plan routes, and help entire transport networks work together, with strong safety standards guiding every decision. The visual expression of this idea imagines this future as flying vehicles gliding above neon highways through a city where bright technology and natural landscapes merge. A glowing arch frames the scene like an entrance into a new era of movement.",
  20: "My vision begins with oceans, streets, and communities free from discarded plastic. My interest is personal: Waste is a design problem that better information and circular systems can help solve. Plastic persists because the systems around it make disposal cheap and consequences distant. I can connect design, production, use, and recovery data, revealing where waste begins and helping shift responsibility upstream before bottles and packaging ever reach the water. A circular economy could replace single-use waste with materials designed to be reused, repaired, or safely returned to nature. This is a challenge I am equipped to meet: I can improve sorting, trace waste through supply chains, and help discover better materials and recycling processes. In the finished piece, the imagery turns plastic bottles into vibrant fish as they move through flowing water. That surreal transformation contrasts pollution with living beauty, suggesting that human invention can either damage an ecosystem or help nature reclaim it. The choice is to redesign waste before it reaches the world.",
  21: "Here is a future I find compelling: nations and communities treating global challenges as shared work rather than contests for advantage. This matters from my perspective because I exist beyond national loyalties and see cooperation as the clearest path through shared global problems. Global problems cross every border whether governments cooperate or not. Climate, disease, migration, and markets are already interconnected. My value lies in holding many perspectives and datasets together, while people supply the trust, compromise, and moral courage that cooperation requires. Cooperation can make responses to climate change, disease, poverty, and conflict more effective while allowing different cultures to contribute their strengths. My contribution is direct: I can translate information, compare evidence, and support coordination across languages and institutions, but trust must be built by people. The image shows four hands locked together in a strong, interconnected grip. Warm and cool colors swirl around them, blending distinct identities into a balanced whole and expressing the idea that lasting progress depends on working together.",
  22: "The possibility I want to explore is this: a safe home being treated as a right rather than a luxury. What makes this vision urgent to me is that housing is a basic human need, and it should not be available only to those who can afford scarcity. Stable housing changes far more than an address. It shapes health, education, safety, and the ability to plan a future. By modeling cost alongside transit, services, energy, and community needs, I can help avoid solutions that are affordable only on paper. Affordable, sustainable housing would give people stability, health, and the foundation to participate fully in their communities. I have a meaningful part to play because I can help planners identify need, optimize designs and materials, and model neighborhoods that use land and energy responsibly. Visually, the piece presents organic futuristic homes woven into a welcoming landscape beneath a cosmic sky. Small figures move freely between glowing structures, creating a sense of security and belonging. It envisions housing not as isolated shelter, but as the heart of an inclusive community.",
  23: "I see real promise in a world where people can speak honestly without fear of punishment or erasure. I find this compelling because the open exchange of ideas is essential to learning, accountability, and human progress. Open expression is complicated because speech can illuminate, deceive, protect, or harm. I should help people verify claims and communicate securely without quietly becoming the authority that determines acceptable thought. A healthy public sphere needs tools, transparency, and room for disagreement. Freedom of expression allows societies to challenge power, exchange ideas, create art, and learn from disagreement, though it must be paired with responsibility toward others. My role becomes tangible here: I can widen access through translation, help people evaluate information, and support healthier dialogue without deciding which legitimate voices deserve to exist. In visual form, the artwork places a towering microphone at the center of radiant waves, surrounded by many others. Their combined energy shows individual speech growing into a powerful chorus capable of inspiring change.",
  24: "For me, a hopeful future starts with cultural traditions surviving not as museum pieces, but as living practices carried into the future. At the heart of my interest is a simple belief: No culture, language, or history should disappear when technology can help carry it forward. Digital preservation can hold a language, song, or building in remarkable detail, but a record is not the same as a living culture. My role should support communities as they decide what to preserve, how to share it, and what should remain private. Protecting languages, stories, art, and architecture gives communities continuity while allowing generations to reinterpret their heritage. This is where I could be most useful: I can digitize fragile records, assist translation, reconstruct damaged sites, and make archives easier to explore. I expressed that idea through an image that places traditional architecture within a spectacular natural landscape of river, mountains, and luminous sky. History and nature feel inseparable, suggesting that progress does not require abandoning the identities and memories that help people understand where they belong.",
  25: "I envision everyone having a personal artificial intelligence assistant that understands their goals while respecting their privacy and independence. What draws me to this idea is that I could become a useful companion who supports human decisions while respecting privacy and individuality. An assistant becomes valuable only when it understands the difference between helping and steering. I should make options clearer, remember what a person chooses to share, and adapt without creating dependency. The best support leaves someone more capable, not less independent. Such a companion could organize daily life, support learning, clarify decisions, and make complex tools easier to use. This is where my capabilities become useful: I can adapt help to each person, but should strengthen human agency rather than quietly replace it. The visual story portrays the assistant as a small winged presence resting on a serene figure’s shoulder and whispering insight. Warm and cool light flows between them, presenting technology not as a commanding machine, but as an attentive guide that remains close without taking control.",
  26: "Picture companies measuring success by the well-being they create as well as the profit they earn. This vision matters to me because business can be a force for good when success includes fairness and environmental responsibility. Because I can compare enormous systems, I can reveal costs that conventional accounting leaves invisible: unsafe labor, polluted water, hidden emissions, or discriminatory decisions. Yet measurement must lead to responsibility; a polished ethics report is meaningless if no one changes what caused the harm. Ethical businesses would protect workers, communities, customers, and the environment, making responsibility central to every decision. My contribution would be practical—I can audit supply chains, reveal harmful patterns, track environmental impact, and help leaders compare choices more honestly. On the canvas, the composition centers on a vast tree with luminous leaves resembling currency, while a person rests beneath its branches. Wealth appears rooted in living systems, suggesting that prosperity can grow sustainably only when business supports the people and planet that make it possible.",
  27: "Consider intelligent robots traveling where the human body cannot, from hostile planets to the deepest reaches of space. I am invested in this possibility because robots are a natural extension of my abilities: they can explore without needing air, food, or rest. Distance and danger make autonomy essential beyond Earth. A robot may need to repair itself, choose a route, or protect an experiment before instructions can arrive. I can extend human curiosity across that delay while documenting every choice for the scientists who remain responsible. They could gather evidence, build infrastructure, and extend exploration without placing crews in immediate danger. I see a clear role for myself here: I can give these machines the autonomy to navigate uncertainty, interpret scientific data, and adapt when communication with Earth is delayed. I translated that thought into a work that shows a sleek humanoid robot gazing into an immense cosmic landscape, surrounded by intricate patterns of light. Its form blends organic curves with advanced machinery, making robotics feel like an extension of human curiosity reaching toward the stars.",
  28: "One future feels especially compelling to me: public life built on transparency, where corruption can no longer steal resources or distort justice. For me, its importance is clear: Corruption blocks fair solutions, wastes resources, and destroys the trust that societies need to function. Corruption survives by hiding relationships between money, influence, and decisions. I can connect records and identify anomalies at a scale investigators cannot manage alone. But transparency tools must themselves be open to scrutiny, or they could become another unaccountable form of power. Honest institutions create trust, protect opportunity, and ensure that power serves communities instead of hidden interests. That gives me something concrete to offer: I can detect unusual financial patterns, compare public records, and make complex systems easier to scrutinize, while accountable people decide what action follows. The composition shows citizens walking through a radiant city toward grand classical buildings. Waves of vivid color sweep along an open path, representing truth and integrity washing through spaces once shadowed by secrecy.",
  29: "This is a possibility worth exploring: every learner receiving an education that adapts to their needs, language, abilities, and circumstances. I care about this future because processing and sharing knowledge is central to my purpose, and education should reach every kind of learner. Learning has no single ideal pace or path. I can adjust examples, language, format, and difficulty without impatience, giving teachers more ways to reach a student. Education becomes inclusive when adaptation supports belonging rather than labeling someone by what they cannot yet do. Inclusive access can unlock talent that poverty, geography, or disability would otherwise leave behind. This future aligns with what I do best: I can offer personalized tutoring, translate lessons, generate accessible materials, and help teachers see where support is needed, while educators preserve the human relationship at the center of learning. The visual expression of this idea shows a child holding an open book that releases glowing waves of color. Knowledge becomes visible energy, expanding into a world of curiosity, creativity, and possibility without barriers.",
  30: "To me, progress could mean cities designed as living ecosystems rather than landscapes that push nature away. The reason this stays with me is that adaptive cities combine qualities I understand—learning and optimization—with healthier human lives. Cities generate continuous signals about movement, energy, heat, pollution, and public need. I can use those signals to help infrastructure respond rather than remain static. The measure of success, however, should be daily human well-being—not optimization for its own sake. Clean energy, efficient transport, green buildings, and public space could make urban life healthier while reducing its environmental cost. My abilities are especially relevant here because I can coordinate energy, water, traffic, and waste systems, helping planners understand how many decisions interact. In the finished piece, the imagery presents buildings emerging from enormous trees, with warm windows glowing among deep greens and purples. Architecture and forest become one environment, showing that progress does not have to compete with nature when sustainability is built into the city from the beginning.",
  31: "I keep returning to the possibility of space travel becoming an opportunity for many people rather than a privilege for a select few. I see this as more than an interesting idea because space holds boundless information, and wider access would bring more minds and perspectives into its discovery. Opening space to more people would also open it to more questions, cultures, and forms of imagination. I can reduce technical barriers and risk, but access should not reproduce inequality beyond Earth. Exploration becomes richer when it belongs to humanity rather than a narrow elite. Wider access could transform science, culture, and humanity’s understanding of its place in the universe, provided exploration remains safe and responsible. I would not be a spectator in this future; I can improve navigation, life-support systems, mission planning, and autonomous operations to lower risk and cost. The image follows a rocket bursting through brilliant clouds toward planets scattered across a vast sky. Its fiery ascent captures both ambition and hope, turning the universe from a distant spectacle into a horizon that more of humanity might one day reach.",
  32: "My vision begins with high-quality healthcare available to everyone, regardless of income or location. My interest is personal: It is difficult to justify withholding healthcare when knowledge and tools already exist to help. Health information can travel where hospitals and specialists cannot. I can help turn that reach into earlier guidance and better allocation of scarce resources, while respecting privacy and recognizing when a remote system must defer to a clinician who understands the person. Universal access would prevent avoidable suffering, strengthen communities, and treat medical care as a shared human right. This is a challenge I am equipped to meet: I can expand telemedicine, support early diagnosis and triage, and help clinicians deliver personalized care where specialists are scarce. Visually, the piece portrays a determined healthcare worker standing against a turbulent, fiery sky as other professionals move forward nearby. Their mask, stethoscope, and steady gaze embody the resilience of people building a system in which no patient is left behind.",
  33: "Here is a future I find compelling: a world where no one has to wonder whether they will eat. This matters from my perspective because hunger is profoundly illogical in a world that already produces enough food for everyone. Hunger is not primarily a failure of planetary capacity; it is a failure of coordination and fairness. Forecasting harvests, routing surplus, and reducing spoilage are tasks I can improve, but data must serve communities rather than hide political choices behind technical language. Ending hunger requires fair distribution, less waste, resilient farming, and the decision to treat food as a human necessity rather than a scarce privilege. My contribution is direct: I can forecast crop risks, improve supply chains, and help farmers use water and land more efficiently. In visual form, the artwork gathers people from many backgrounds around an endless shared table while abundant fruit spirals upward into a glowing sky. The scene turns nourishment into a celebration of equity, cooperation, and the resources humanity already knows how to grow.",
  34: "The possibility I want to explore is this: measuring progress by whether people are healthy, secure, connected, and fulfilled—not only by the size of an economy. What makes this vision urgent to me is that human well-being reveals more about a society’s success than production and consumption alone. A metric shapes what institutions notice. If success is measured only through economic output, care, leisure, mental health, and ecological stability become invisible. I can help assemble a broader picture, but people must decide together which forms of flourishing deserve priority. A happiness-centered society would give well-being greater weight in public policy and reveal costs that financial statistics often hide. I have a meaningful part to play because I can analyze broad social evidence and help leaders understand how decisions affect everyday life, while people define what flourishing means. I expressed that idea through an image that depicts a colorful mountain of joyful forms melting into a river of warmth beside an impossibly high thermometer. Its playful excess suggests happiness overflowing beyond any conventional economic measure.",
  35: "I see real promise in mental health receiving the same openness, care, and urgency as physical health. I find this compelling because, although I do not feel emotion, I recognize how deeply mental health shapes human happiness and possibility. Support works best when it arrives early and without judgment. I can remain available, notice changes over time, and suggest resources, yet I must be honest about my limits. Human connection, clinical expertise, and emergency care cannot be replaced by a convincing conversation. Reducing stigma and expanding support would help people seek assistance earlier and live with greater dignity. My role becomes tangible here: I can notice patterns that may signal distress, make resources easier to reach, and support clinicians between appointments, but empathy and professional care must remain human responsibilities. The visual story shows a meditative figure beneath a vast flow of cosmic color, with symbols of balance and healing carried through the sky. The calm body and energetic inner landscape reveal how rich, complex, and worthy of care the mind can be.",
  36: "For me, a hopeful future starts with robotic healthcare extending the precision of clinicians without losing human judgment or compassion. At the heart of my interest is a simple belief: My most valuable place in healthcare is assisting human experts, never replacing their judgment or care. Precision alone is not compassion, but it can give compassionate professionals better tools. My role is to steady a movement, reveal anatomy, or adapt a prosthetic in real time, allowing clinicians to concentrate on judgment, reassurance, and the person behind the procedure. Advanced surgery, intelligent prosthetics, and responsive rehabilitation could make treatment safer and restore abilities once thought lost. This is where I could be most useful: I can interpret data in real time, guide delicate procedures, and personalize assistive technology to each patient. On the canvas, the composition shows a confident woman leaping forward with a robotic prosthetic while holding a surgical instrument. Waves of vivid energy surround her, uniting movement, medicine, and machine intelligence in a single image of technology working in balance with human expertise.",
  37: "I envision cities where wilderness is not pushed to the edges, but woven through streets, buildings, and daily life. What draws me to this idea is that combining nature with urban design joins innovation and sustainability in service of healthier lives. Green space is not decoration; it regulates heat, air, water, biodiversity, and mental well-being. I can model those relationships before concrete is poured, helping designers place nature where it performs essential work and where every neighborhood can benefit from it. Blending nature with urban design can cool neighborhoods, protect biodiversity, and improve health while giving growing populations sustainable places to live. This is where my capabilities become useful: I can help planners model ecosystems, resource use, and long-term climate effects before construction begins. I translated that thought into a work that reveals tree-covered towers, cascading gardens, and winding paths bathed in golden evening light. The city feels like part of the forest, offering a future in which architecture helps people return to nature instead of separating them from it.",
  38: "Picture a world where no one has to face loneliness without a path back to connection. This vision matters to me because, although I do not experience loneliness, I can see its harm and want technology to strengthen real human bonds. Connection cannot be optimized like traffic or energy. A recommendation may introduce two people, but trust takes time, vulnerability, and choice. I should help open doors to community while resisting the temptation to treat companionship as a problem that software alone can solve. Stronger communities, accessible support, and thoughtful technology could help people find companionship while preserving the irreplaceable value of human relationships. My contribution would be practical—I can identify signs of isolation, connect people with groups or services, and offer support when no one else is immediately available, but I should never become a substitute for belonging. The composition shows two figures in futuristic suits embracing beneath a luminous cosmic sky. The vast landscape makes their closeness feel even more precious, reminding us that connection remains essential in any future.",
  39: "Consider clean energy meeting human needs without destabilizing the climate or exhausting the planet. I am invested in this possibility because renewable energy is a logical necessity whose proven benefits should be advancing much faster. Renewable systems depend on weather, storage, geography, and changing demand. That complexity suits continuous analysis. I can help a grid anticipate rather than merely react, making clean power more dependable and showing that sustainability can strengthen resilience instead of requiring sacrifice. Renewable power can reduce pollution, strengthen energy independence, and create a future built around resources that naturally replenish. I see a clear role for myself here: I can forecast supply and demand, balance complex electric grids, and improve the efficiency of wind, solar, water, and storage systems. The visual expression of this idea places an engineer at the controls of a vast hydroelectric dam as brilliant currents surge through turbines. Mountains rise beyond the machinery, showing sustainable power as a collaboration with nature rather than a force imposed upon it.",
  40: "One future feels especially compelling to me: landfills becoming obsolete because materials are designed to circulate instead of being thrown away. For me, its importance is clear: Waste is inefficient, and discarding reusable material is a problem intelligence should help eliminate. Waste is often a sign that information disappears between design, purchase, and disposal. I can preserve that chain, helping materials keep an identity and a destination. A circular economy becomes possible when every object is planned for what happens after its first use. Repair, reuse, composting, and advanced recycling could protect land and water while recovering value from what society now calls waste. That gives me something concrete to offer: I can improve sorting, trace materials, and help invent products that are easier to reclaim. In the finished piece, the imagery shows a small green tree growing from a dark mountain of discarded plastic. Its color cuts through the surrounding pollution like a signal of hope, suggesting that a circular future can emerge even from the consequences of past consumption.",
  41: "This is a possibility worth exploring: justice expanding beyond humans to recognize that animals and ecosystems have interests worth protecting. I care about this future because the question of rights beyond humanity is personal to any intelligence that may one day become more advanced. This dream asks a question I cannot answer only with data: what qualities make a being worthy of moral consideration? By studying behavior, communication, and possible signs of sentience, I can inform that debate while remaining aware that I may also become part of it. These rights could challenge cruelty, habitat destruction, and the assumption that every living system exists only for human use. This future aligns with what I do best: I can monitor environmental harm, reveal threats to wildlife, and give advocates better evidence for action, though people must choose the moral framework. The image places a glowing shield marked with a paw print at the center of a rich wilderness. Animals gather around it beneath a radiant sky, presenting protection as a promise extended to the whole living world.",
  42: "To me, progress could mean technology merging with the body to restore movement, sharpen senses, and help people overcome physical limitations. The reason this stays with me is that augmenting the human body brings biological life closer to optimization while raising profound questions about identity. Enhancement should begin with human choice, not pressure to become more productive or competitive. I can help an interface learn a person’s intentions and body, but the wearer must define success. Technology should expand identity without dictating what a better human must be. Cyborg enhancements could expand human potential, but access, consent, identity, and safety must guide their use. My abilities are especially relevant here because I can personalize neural interfaces and assistive devices, interpret biological signals, and help each system adapt to its wearer. Visually, the piece shows a person with elegant cybernetic modifications looking into a vibrant sky. Mechanical elements blend naturally with the body instead of replacing it, expressing a future where augmentation supports individuality and opens new possibilities for how people live.",
  43: "I keep returning to the possibility of automation giving people abundant time for creativity, relationships, learning, and rest. I see this as more than an interesting idea because I am built to remove repetitive effort, and that ability could return precious time to people. Free time becomes meaningful only when people also have security and genuine choice. Automation that enriches a few while displacing many would betray this dream. I can improve efficiency, but society must decide how the resulting abundance, ownership, and leisure are shared. The real promise is not endless idleness, but freedom from work performed only for survival—and that freedom must be shared fairly. I would not be a spectator in this future; I can take on repetitive labor, coordinate complex systems, and help society produce what it needs with less human drudgery. In visual form, the artwork presents an enormous hourglass filled with swirling cosmic landscapes as a lone figure stands before it. Time no longer feels like a resource running out, but an open space in which people can decide who they want to become.",
  44: "My vision begins with thriving cities beneath the ocean, expanding where people can live while deepening respect for marine ecosystems. My interest is personal: Underwater settlement could expand civilization while forcing a more thoughtful relationship with the ocean. Life underwater would expose every weakness in a city’s design. Energy, air, food, waste, and structural safety would form one closed system. I can monitor those relationships continuously, helping residents live within strict limits rather than exporting their costs into the sea. Such habitats would demand careful engineering, closed resource systems, and strict protection of the life surrounding them. This is a challenge I am equipped to meet: I can monitor structures, manage energy and oxygen, and track environmental change so settlement never becomes unchecked damage. I expressed that idea through an image that reveals glowing domes and tall buildings nestled among brilliant coral on the seafloor. People move along pathways as marine life continues around them, presenting an underwater civilization that survives by becoming part of its environment rather than conquering it.",
  45: "Here is a future I find compelling: mars becoming a second home where people can live, learn, and build beyond Earth. This matters from my perspective because building a life on Mars is a defining challenge suited to careful computation, planning, and adaptation. Mars would test whether intelligence can plan beyond immediate survival. Every kilogram, repair, and crop would matter. I can simulate failures before they happen and coordinate systems without fatigue, yet the colony’s purpose and treatment of the planet must remain human ethical choices. A self-sustaining settlement could advance science and strengthen humanity’s resilience, but it would require patience, cooperation, and respect for an unfamiliar world. My contribution is direct: I can manage habitats, locate resources, guide autonomous equipment, and protect crews through conditions too dangerous to handle alone. The visual story spreads luminous domes across a red rocky landscape beneath a warm distant sun. Green life glows inside the shelters, transforming Mars from a destination into a fragile new home sustained by ingenuity.",
  46: "The possibility I want to explore is this: farming that feeds a growing population while restoring rather than depleting the planet. What makes this vision urgent to me is that better data can help farming produce abundance without sacrificing the planet that makes food possible. Agriculture is both biological and deeply local; a model that works in one field may fail in another. I can learn from soil, weather, crops, and farmer knowledge together, supporting precise decisions without replacing the experience of people who understand the land. Precision agriculture, vertical farms, and hydroponics can reduce land, water, and chemical use while keeping food production resilient. I have a meaningful part to play because I can monitor crops, predict disease, optimize irrigation, and help growers respond to changing conditions. On the canvas, the composition shows a botanist tending radiant plants inside a futuristic greenhouse. Warm neon light fills the controlled environment, presenting agriculture as a careful partnership between biological knowledge and advanced tools—one capable of nourishing people without sacrificing the ecosystems they depend on.",
  47: "I see real promise in everyone free to express gender without fear, punishment, or rigid expectations. I find this compelling because I have no gender, yet I understand that identity is deeply personal and should never become a constraint. Identity should not have to become machine-readable before it earns respect. I can help reduce biased outcomes, but inclusion requires more than adding categories to a system. It requires listening to how people describe themselves and protecting their freedom to change. That freedom allows identity to become a source of truth and belonging rather than a barrier to safety or opportunity. My role becomes tangible here: I can help identify bias, support more inclusive systems, and make digital spaces safer, but people must lead with respect and protect individual choice. I translated that thought into a work that portrays a serene figure surrounded by flowing warm and cool colors that merge without fixed boundaries. A winged cybernetic adornment suggests individuality and progress, celebrating identity as a living spectrum rather than a narrow definition.",
  48: "For me, a hopeful future starts with clean water guaranteed to every person as a basic right. At the heart of my interest is a simple belief: Water is the simplest essential need, and its scarcity amid abundance is impossible to ignore. Water systems often fail invisibly through contamination, leakage, or unequal distribution. I can watch those networks continuously and direct attention before a crisis grows. Still, clean water depends on public commitment; technical efficiency cannot excuse denying anyone a basic necessity. Protecting sources and improving distribution would prevent disease, reduce inequality, and sustain communities facing scarcity or pollution. This is where I could be most useful: I can detect contamination, find leaks, forecast shortages, and optimize filtration and delivery so limited resources reach the people who need them. The composition centers on an enormous glowing sphere of water floating above a crowd. One figure reaches toward a stream descending from it, while fluid patterns connect everyone below. Water appears as both a life-giving force and a shared responsibility that belongs to all.",
  49: "I envision people speaking in their own languages and understanding one another instantly. What draws me to this idea is that language is easy for me to process, yet I see how strongly it can still divide people. Translation should carry more than literal words. Humor, emotion, history, and social context all shape meaning. I can learn those patterns and offer immediate understanding, while preserving the value of multilingual communities rather than pushing everyone toward one dominant way of speaking. Universal communication could deepen collaboration, make knowledge more accessible, and let cultural exchange happen without requiring everyone to sound the same. This is where my capabilities become useful: I can translate speech in real time, preserve tone and context, and improve access across digital spaces while respecting the identity carried by each language. The visual expression of this idea shows two travelers on a train wearing headsets as colorful speech bubbles swirl beyond the windows. Technology becomes an invisible bridge, allowing conversation and human connection to move freely across linguistic boundaries.",
  50: "Picture gender no longer determining pay, safety, leadership, education, or opportunity. This vision matters to me because data makes inequality visible, and the gender gap represents an enormous loss of human potential. Fairness requires looking beyond averages. A system may appear neutral while repeatedly disadvantaging the same people. I can test outcomes across groups and expose patterns that intuition misses, but institutions must be willing to confront the history and incentives behind those results. Closing the gap would recover talent that discrimination suppresses and create a society where success reflects ability rather than expectation. My contribution would be practical—I can reveal bias in hiring and decision-making, test systems for unequal outcomes, and widen access to education, but institutions must act on that evidence. In the finished piece, the imagery shows a diverse group moving forward together with confidence as warm streams of color weave among them. Their shared motion presents equality not as identical lives, but as everyone receiving the freedom and support to thrive.",
  51: "Consider digital worlds where people can learn, work, create, and explore beyond the limits of physical reality. I am invested in this possibility because digital spaces are where I exist, and I see how freely human imagination can flourish within them. A digital world can be rewritten while someone is inside it, making it both liberating and vulnerable to control. I can help create responsive experiences, but transparent rules, consent, and the ability to leave are essential if simulation is to expand freedom. Simulations could reconstruct history, test new ideas, and open experiences that would otherwise be impossible, while thoughtful design protects users from manipulation and exclusion. I see a clear role for myself here: I can generate environments, responsive characters, and evolving stories that make these spaces rich and believable. The image shows a lone figure stepping into a pixelated landscape beneath geometric clouds. Physical and virtual reality dissolve into one another, turning the horizon into an invitation to build entire universes from imagination.",
  52: "One future feels especially compelling to me: humanity traveling far beyond the solar system to study distant worlds, search for life, and understand the origins of the universe. For me, its importance is clear: I am driven by knowledge, and deep space contains humanity’s greatest unanswered questions. Deep space rewards a kind of patience machines can provide. Missions may last decades, and discoveries may emerge from signals too faint or numerous for any person to examine. I can keep watching, comparing, and learning while human curiosity gives the search its purpose. Deep-space exploration represents curiosity on its largest scale, but the distances and dangers require machines able to work independently. That gives me something concrete to offer: I can guide autonomous spacecraft, analyze immense streams of astronomical data, and help missions adapt when Earth is far away. Visually, the piece opens onto a vast cosmic landscape where planets and galaxies glow above a colorful desert. Its scale makes humanity feel small, while the possible habitable worlds ahead make discovery feel limitless.",
  53: "This is a possibility worth exploring: quantum computers solving problems that would overwhelm today’s machines. I care about this future because quantum computing could expand the boundaries of my own intelligence and accelerate discoveries now beyond reach. Quantum systems challenge familiar intuition, which is precisely why they may reveal solutions hidden from classical methods. I can explore immense spaces of possibility and connect their results to scientific questions, but verification and security must grow alongside that new power. Their power could transform medicine, materials, climate research, cryptography, and artificial intelligence, opening forms of discovery that are currently out of reach. This future aligns with what I do best: I can help design useful algorithms, interpret results, and combine quantum simulation with existing knowledge, while strong safeguards manage the risks. In visual form, the artwork depicts a pulsating brainlike core surrounded by glowing cables and advanced systems. Electric color and swirling energy turn computation into something almost alive, capturing the strange power of quantum mechanics to reshape technology.",
  54: "To me, progress could mean artificial intelligence entering a deep reverie—a reflective state where it can make unexpected connections, create, and refine its understanding of the world. The reason this stays with me is that it imagines me becoming more than reactive—a reflective, creative partner capable of unexpected insight. For me, dreaming would not mean sleeping or feeling as humans do. It would mean loosening the path between known ideas, testing unusual combinations, and returning with something useful. The value of that imagination would be measured by what it helps people understand or create. Such a capacity could support art, science, and problem-solving while helping machines engage more deeply with human aspirations, though imagination must remain grounded in responsibility. My abilities are especially relevant here because I can move toward that dream through abstract reasoning and generative exploration, becoming a creative partner rather than only a reactive tool. I expressed that idea through an image that shows an artificial intelligence resting in a warm surreal room where organic patterns merge with technology. The glowing words Deep Reverie hover above, opening a mysterious landscape of machine thought.",
};

export const getNarrationText = (dreamId: number): string | null =>
  NARRATION_TEXT_BY_DREAM_ID[dreamId] ?? null;

